; Vec - a growable array of machine words, in Axiom.
;
; A `Vec` is the address of a four-word header:
;
;     word 0 : len  - number of live elements
;     word 1 : cap  - elements the data block can hold
;     word 2 : data - address of cap * 8 bytes of element storage
;     word 3 : kind - what the vector claims about its elements (below)
;
; Elements are machine words. Because Axiom's representation is uniform -
; every value is one 64-bit word - the same `Vec` code stores `Int`s,
; `Str` handles, and boxed `data` values with no monomorphisation. One
; body serves every element type, and `(Vec a)` is a type parameter over
; that one body rather than a family of them.
;
; A vector whose slots genuinely hold different kinds of thing - the
; heterogeneous records this compiler builds - uses the accessors that
; pair a cast with a share: `vecPushStr`, `vecPushVec`, `vecGetStr`,
; `vecGetVec`.
;
; The header is mutated in place, so a `Vec` handle is stable for the
; lifetime of the vector: only `data` is ever reallocated, and it lives
; behind the header. That is the property `Intern` depends on, and it is
; why the mutating operations here can be called in statement position.
;
; OWNERSHIP. A `(Vec a)` is a counted value like a `String` or a
; struct: the compiler releases it when its last reference goes - at
; scope end, when a `mut` slot holding it is overwritten, or when the
; block holding it dies. The header's reference map names word 2, so
; the data block goes with it, and growth hands the abandoned block
; back to the allocator.
;
; The ELEMENTS go too when their type is counted. Nothing at
; construction knows the element type - `vecNew` is one body for every
; `a` - so the vector learns it from the first element it is given.
; `vecPush` takes its share of `x` through `__retainref`, which answers
; whether `x`'s type at that call is counted. Word 3 records the answer:
;
;   0  nothing stored yet
;   1  every element is a counted value this vector holds a share of.
;      The data block carries the array form (`Mem.memMarkArray`), so
;      the vector's death releases each element, and `vecSet`,
;      `vecClear` and `vecPop` hand back or transfer what they remove.
;   2  plain words the vector makes no claim about: `Int`s, or a
;      heterogeneous record. A counted value stored here keeps the
;      share its store took, and is never released.
;
; A vector that owns its elements and is then given a word whose type
; is not counted at that call - an `Int` through `vecPushStr`'s
; heterogeneous record, or a value whose type the call cannot see -
; becomes plain. The data block reverts to a leaf, so no word is
; released that no share was taken for, and the shares already taken
; stay with their elements. That is the leak direction, never an early
; free.
(import Mem)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Construction
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The capacity `vecNew` starts with.
;
; Eight, because the vectors a compiler creates most often - an argument
; list, a scope frame, a constructor's field list - are small enough that
; eight never grows, and the header plus eight words is 88 bytes, which
; is a single bump of the allocator. Starting at 1 or 2 would make those
; cases pay two or three reallocations each, and every reallocation leaks
; (see the module comment).
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecDefaultCap Int)
(fn (vecDefaultCap)
  8)

; An empty `Vec` with `vecDefaultCap` capacity.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecNew (Vec a))
(pub fn (vecNew)
  (vecWithCapacity vecDefaultCap))

; An empty `Vec` that can hold at least `cap` elements without growing.
;
; `cap` is clamped up to 1 rather than honoured as 0. A zero capacity
; would be a real trap here: growth is `cap * 2`, and doubling zero is
; zero, so a zero-capacity vector would recurse forever the first time it
; was pushed to. Clamping at the constructor means `vecGrownCap` can rely
; on `cap >= 1` and needs no special case.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecWithCapacity (-> Int (Vec a)))
(pub fn (vecWithCapacity cap)
  (vecBuild cap))

; The bytes `n` elements take. Above 2^59 elements the product is past
; the 2^62 bytes an allocation can ask for (MM-ALLOC-7a), and at 2^61 it
; wrapped to 0: `vecWithCapacity` asked for an empty block, answered a
; vector claiming 2^61 slots, and its pushes wrote over whatever the
; allocator handed out next. Such a count answers -1, which the
; allocator refuses before any arithmetic: out of memory, status 70,
; recoverable (ERR-REC-6; `tests/stdlib/562-vec-capacity-range.ax`).
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecBytes (-> Int Int))
(fn (vecBytes n)
  (if (> n 576460752303423488)
    (- 0 1)
    (* n 8)))

; The header and its data block. The data block is retained once here:
; `memAlloc` answers a count of zero, which release reads as "nobody
; owns this", so without the header's share the header's map could not
; reclaim it.
;
; THE HEADER IS NOT RETAINED HERE. The cast to `(Vec a)` at the return
; is a cast to a counted type in return position, which the compiler
; retains: that is the share the caller receives, as `Map.mapAllocTable`
; describes for its header.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: vecBuild (-> Int (Vec a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (vecBuild cap)
  (let (
    (n (if (< cap 1)
      1
      cap))
    (v (memAllocMapped 32 4))
    (d (memAlloc (vecBytes n)))
  )
    {
      (__retain d)
      (memSetWord v 0 0)
      (memSetWord v 1 n)
      (memSetWord v 2 d)
      ; WORD 3 IS THE VECTOR'S OWN RECORD of what it holds, and not a
      ; read of the data block's array bit: only the compiler that
      ; emits the release walk agrees with the one that wrote the bit
      ; about where the count field ends (`stdlib/Mem.ax`), so the
      ; vector says what it is in a word of its own.
      (memSetWord v 3 0)
      (cast (Vec a) v)
    }))

; Hand `v` back early. Its data block goes with it, and, when the
; vector owns its elements, so does one share of each. The caller must
; own the share being released and must not use the handle again: the
; compiler releases a `Vec` at the end of its scope, so a caller that
; simply stops using one needs nothing here.
;
; Answers 0 rather than the handle: a freed handle is the one thing this
; module must not encourage a caller to keep using.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecFree (-> (Vec a) Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecFree v)
  {
    (__release
      (cast Int v))
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Accessors
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Whether this vector holds a share of every element - kind 1 in the
; module comment. It reads word 3 of the header, not the data block's
; shape word: see `vecBuild`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecOwnsRefs (-> (Vec a) Bool))
(pub fn (vecOwnsRefs v)
  (==
    (memGetWord
      (cast Int v)
      3)
    1))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecLen (-> (Vec a) Int))
(pub fn (vecLen v)
  (memGetWord
    (cast Int v)
    0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecCap (-> (Vec a) Int))
(pub fn (vecCap v)
  (memGetWord
    (cast Int v)
    1))

; The address of the element block.
;
; Exposed because `Intern` and any other module that wants to walk a
; vector without paying a bounds check per element needs it, and because
; hiding it would be pretend safety: the handle is an `Int`, so a caller
; that wants the data pointer can already read word 2 itself.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecData (-> (Vec a) Int))
(fn (vecData v)
  (memGetWord
    (cast Int v)
    2))

; The element at `i`. REFUSES an index outside `0 .. (vecLen v) - 1`.
;
; TWO CLAIMS USED TO STAND HERE AND BOTH ARE GONE. They are written out
; because every caller in this tree was built against them.
;
; THE SENTINEL. Out of range answered 0 rather than trapping until
; 2026-09-01, matching `Str.strByte`, on the argument that Axiom had no
; way to signal failure that did not either allocate - the builtin
; `Option` works, but every `data` value is heap-boxed - or abort the
; process. The cost was stated and it was the wrong side of the trade:
; 0 IS A VALUE A CALLER MAY HAVE PUSHED, so a read past the end and a
; read of a stored zero came back the same word and no caller could
; tell them apart. That is not a crash, it is a wrong answer that keeps
; going, and on a parser fed by a peer it is the worse failure. Out of
; range is `(__indexTrap)` now - a call that never returns, status 77 -
; and `vecTry` below is the checked read for a caller that has a
; question rather than an invariant.
;
; THE ELEMENT TYPE. This was declared `(-> Int Int Int)` over a bare
; handle, and before that it answered a type VARIABLE over one - which
; is a stronger claim than a bare handle can support and a false one:
; the caller named any type it liked and got it, so `(vecPush v 42)`
; followed by `(strWrap (vecGet v 0) 8)` type-checked and dereferenced
; 42 as a String. `AX3040` closed that by pinning the answer to `Int`,
; at the price of a `cast` on every read of anything else.
;
; `a` is the VECTOR's element type now, not the caller's wish: a
; `(Vec String)` reads back `String` with no cast at the call site and
; a `(Vec Int)` reads back `Int`. That is a fact about the container,
; not a guarantee about what a word can be made to mean - measured on
; this tree, `(vecPush v 42)` with `(strWrap (vecGet v 0) 8)` still
; compiles, because both sides really are `Int` and `strWrap` is the
; one lying. What ended is the vector that could not say what it held.
;
; A vector whose slots genuinely hold different KINDS of thing - the
; heterogeneous records this compiler builds - still needs a cast to
; read one back, and it takes the paired accessor that says so at the
; call in one word: `vecGetStr`, `vecGetVec`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecGet (-> (Vec a) Int a))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecGet v i)
  (if (< i 0)
    __indexTrap
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      __indexTrap
      (cast a (memGetWord (vecData v) i)))))

; The element at `i`, or `None` when there is no element at `i`.
;
; `vecGet` above REFUSES an out-of-range index - status 77, via
; `(__indexTrap)`. It used to answer 0, and 0 IS A VALUE A CALLER MAY
; HAVE PUSHED - so a caller who reads past the end and one
; who reads a stored zero receive the same answer and cannot tell which
; happened. On a parser fed by a peer that is the worse of the two
; failure modes: not a crash, a wrong answer that keeps going.
;
; `Option` is built in and needs no import, so this costs `Vec` no new
; dependency - which is why the refusal is spelled this way rather than
; as a `Result`, since `Err` would drag a module in for a distinction
; that has no error to report.
;
; `vecGet` stays, and is still the right call where the index is
; already known good - a loop bounded by `vecLen`. What changed is what
; it does when the index is NOT good: it refuses instead of answering.
; The old sentence continued: "and making every
; such read allocate an `Option` would be a cost paid everywhere to
; describe a case that cannot arise there.
; `no-alloc` came off on 2026-08-31. The comment above already said it -
; "making every such read allocate an `Option`" is what `(Some ...)`
; does here - and the tag claimed the opposite, because a constructor
; application was invisible to the effect row until that date
; (`MM-EXEC-9a`). Returning a sentinel instead would delete the
; distinction this function exists to draw, so the CLAIM is what goes.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecTry (-> (Vec a) Int (Option a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecTry v i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      None
      (Some
        (cast a (memGetWord (vecData v) i))))))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecGetStr (-> (Vec a) Int String))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecGetStr v i)
  (cast String (vecGet v i)))

; The element at `i` read back as a CONTAINER.
;
; A vector whose slots hold different kinds of thing - a `ctx` whose
; slot 0 is an interner and whose slots 1..3 are vectors - cannot be
; given one element type, and the alternative is a `cast` at the call
; site. That is the one thing `docs/memory-model.md` MM-VAL-22 measures
; as unsafe: a cast at an argument root in a type-variable position
; classifies the value's evidence 0 and drops its retain. So the cast
; is HERE, at a return inside a signature that carries the type, which
; is the same shape `vecGetStr` and `memGetWordVec` already use.
;
; The result variable is `b` and not `a`: what the vector holds and
; what this slot holds are different questions, and each call site
; pins `b` for itself.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecGetVec (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec b)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecGetVec v i)
  (cast (Vec b) (vecGet v i)))

; Append a `String` to a vector of WORDS, keeping the share.
;
; Some of this compiler's vectors are heterogeneous by construction -
; a query answer whose slot 0 is a `String` and whose slot 1 is a
; vector - so no single element type describes them and the element
; has to be stored as the word it is. Writing `(vecPush r (cast Int s))`
; at the call site would type-check and would be a USE-AFTER-FREE:
; `vecPush`'s element parameter is a type variable, and MM-VAL-22
; measures that a cast at an argument root there classifies the value's
; evidence 0, so `vecPush`'s `__retainref` emits nothing and the
; vector holds a `String` whose share nobody took.
;
; So the retain is made explicit and the cast is paired with it, in one
; place, behind a signature that says `String`. The count is the same
; as a `(Vec String)` push: one retain here, and the one `vecPush`
; would have taken is exactly the one the cast suppresses. The word
; arrives uncounted, so the vector is plain: it makes no claim on a
; heterogeneous record, and the share taken here is never handed back.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecPushStr (-> (Vec a) String (Vec a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecPushStr v s)
  {
    (__retainref s)
    (vecPush
      v
      (cast a s))
  })

; The same for a nested container. A `Vec` handle is a counted block
; too, so it needs the same explicit share for the same reason.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecPushVec (-> (Vec a) (Vec b) (Vec a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecPushVec v x)
  {
    (__retainref x)
    (vecPush
      v
      (cast a x))
  })

; Overwrite the element at `i`. Returns the handle.
;
; Invalid indices trap with status 77 before touching the vector, just
; as `vecGet` does. A caller that meant to append should say `vecPush`;
; silently dropping a write lets a failed update look successful.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecSet (-> (Vec a) Int a (Vec a)))
(pub fn (vecSet v i x)
  (if (< i 0)
    __indexTrap
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      __indexTrap
      ; The incoming share is taken first, so setting a slot to what
      ; it already holds is safe: the count goes up before the
      ; displaced element's comes down. An owner hands the displaced
      ; share back, which is a leak the walk could never find if it
      ; were dropped with the word. An owner given an uncounted word
      ; becomes plain instead, as `vecPush` describes.
      (let ((counted (__retainref x)))
        {
          (if (vecOwnsRefs v)
            (if (== counted 1)
              (vecDropAt v i)
              {
                (memMarkLeaf (vecData v))
                (memSetWord
                  (cast Int v)
                  3
                  2)
                v
              })
            v)
          (memSetWord
            (vecData v)
            i
            (cast Int x))
          v
        }))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Growth
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Ensure `v` can hold `need` elements, reallocating if it cannot.
; Returns the handle.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: vecReserve (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec a)))
(fn (vecReserve v need)
  (if (<= need (vecCap v))
    v
    (vecReserveExactly v (vecGrownCap (vecCap v) need))))

; The first power-of-two multiple of `cap` that reaches `need`.
;
; Growth factor 2, not 1.5. The usual argument for 1.5 is that it lets an
; allocator reuse the blocks freed by earlier growth steps; with a bump
; allocator and no `free` there is nothing to reuse, so the only thing
; 1.5 buys is more reallocations and more leaked blocks. 2 keeps the
; total bytes copied across n pushes under 2n and the number of
; reallocations at log2(n).
;
; Terminates because `vecWithCapacity` guarantees `cap >= 1`.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecGrownCap (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (vecGrownCap cap need)
  (if (>= cap need)
    cap
    (vecGrownCap (* cap 2) need)))

; Move `v`'s elements into a fresh block of exactly `newCap` elements.
;
; Only `len` elements are copied, not `cap`: the words past `len` are
; unreachable through `vecGet`, so copying them would be pure cost. The
; new block comes from `memAlloc`, which zeroes, so the tail is 0 rather
; than whatever the arena last held there.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
(:: vecReserveExactly (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (vecReserveExactly v newCap)
  (let (
    (old (vecData v))
    (bigger (memAlloc (vecBytes newCap)))
  )
    {
      (__retain bigger)
      (if (vecOwnsRefs v)
        (memMarkArray bigger newCap)
        bigger)
      (memCopy bigger old (* (vecLen v) 8))
      (let ((h (cast Int v)))
        {
          (memSetWord h 2 bigger)
          (memSetWord h 1 newCap)
        })
      ; The copy MOVED the elements' shares, so the old block must not
      ; spend them on the way out: it is marked a leaf first, and its
      ; release then reclaims the block and touches nothing it held.
      ; Without that line a ref vector that grew once would free every
      ; element it still contained.
      (memMarkLeaf old)
      (__release old)
      v
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stack operations
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Append `x`. Returns the handle - the same one, with this
; representation; see the module comment for why it is returned anyway.
;
; `vecData` is read *after* `vecReserve` runs, because reserving may have
; replaced the data pointer. Reading it into a `let` before the reserve
; would write the element into the abandoned block, and the bug would be
; invisible until the vector happened to grow.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-foreign)
;
; The share of `x` is taken here, by `__retainref`, which also answers
; whether `x`'s type at this call is counted. That answer settles what
; the vector claims before the word goes in (word 3, see the module
; comment):
;
; - The first element decides. A counted one makes the vector an
;   owner, and its data block takes the array form: nothing has been
;   stored yet, so every word of the block is the zero `memAlloc`
;   wrote, and the walk may release the whole block. Any other first
;   element makes the vector plain.
; - An owner given an uncounted word becomes plain, and its block a
;   leaf again: the walk would otherwise release a word no share was
;   taken for. The elements it already holds keep their shares and are
;   never released.
(pub :: vecPush (-> (Vec a) a (Vec a)))
(pub fn (vecPush v x)
  (let (
    (len (vecLen v))
    (counted (__retainref x))
    (h (cast Int v))
  )
    (let ((kind (memGetWord h 3)))
      {
        (vecReserve v (+ len 1))
        (if (== kind 0)
          (if (== counted 1)
            {
              (memMarkArray (vecData v) (vecCap v))
              (memSetWord h 3 1)
            }
            (memSetWord h 3 2))
          (if (&& (== kind 1) (== counted 0))
            {
              (memMarkLeaf (vecData v))
              (memSetWord h 3 2)
            }
            0))
        (memSetWord
          (vecData v)
          len
          (cast Int x))
        (memSetWord h 0 (+ len 1))
        v
      })))

; Remove and return the last element. REFUSES an empty vector.
;
; This answered 0 on empty until the element-type port, and the
; ambiguity was `vecGet`'s exactly: 0 is a value a caller may have
; pushed, so a pop off the end and a pop of a stored zero came back the
; same word. The old comment's answer was a rule for the reader -
; "callers that can push 0 must guard with `vecLen`" - which is a rule
; nothing checks and every caller eventually forgets. An empty pop is
; `(__indexTrap)` now, status 77; a caller that does not know whether
; there is an element still asks `vecLen`, and now that is the only way
; to get an answer at all.
;
; Capacity is not reduced: shrinking would reallocate and leak the larger
; block, so a vector used as a stack settles at its high-water mark and
; then stops allocating entirely, which is what a recursive-descent
; parser wants.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecPop (-> (Vec a) a))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (vecPop v)
  (let ((len (vecLen v)))
    (if (== len 0)
      __indexTrap
      ; The slot is ZEROED, and on a vector that owns its elements that is a
      ; correctness rule rather than tidiness: the array-form walk
      ; releases the whole BLOCK's words, so a stale handle left
      ; above `len` would have its share spent at `vecFree` while
      ; the caller this pop handed it to still holds it. A pop
      ; TRANSFERS the vector's share to the caller; the zero is
      ; what makes the transfer true.
      (let ((top (memGetWord (vecData v) (- len 1))))
        {
          (memSetWord (vecData v) (- len 1) 0)
          (memSetWord
            (cast Int v)
            0
            (- len 1))
          (cast a top)
        }))))

; The last element without removing it. REFUSES an empty vector.
;
; It too answered 0 on empty until the element-type port, with the same
; defect as `vecPop`. There is no empty test in the body and none is
; needed: this is `(vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1))`, and on an empty vector
; that index is -1, which `vecGet` refuses.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecLast (-> (Vec a) a))
(pub fn (vecLast v)
  (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1)))

; Drop every element, keeping the capacity. Returns the handle.
;
; The data block is deliberately not zeroed. Nothing can observe the
; stale words - `vecGet` and `vecSum` are both gated on `len` - so
; zeroing would be O(cap) of work to make a debugger's view prettier.
; Keeping the capacity is the point: a `Vec` reused as scratch across
; many iterations allocates once in total.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecClear (-> (Vec a) (Vec a)))
(pub fn (vecClear v)
  {
    (if (vecOwnsRefs v)
      (vecDropFrom v 0)
      v)
    (memSetWord
      (cast Int v)
      0
      0)
    v
  })

; Hand back the share of element `i` and zero the slot. Only ever called
; on an array-form data block, where the vector owns what it holds.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecDropAt (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec a)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (vecDropAt v i)
  (let ((old (memGetWord (vecData v) i)))
    {
      (memSetWord (vecData v) i 0)
      (__release old)
      v
    }))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecDropFrom (-> (Vec a) Int (Vec a)))
(fn (vecDropFrom v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    v
    {
      (vecDropAt v i)
      (vecDropFrom v (+ i 1))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Bulk reads
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The sum of every element.
;
; Exists so a test can check a whole vector's contents with one printed
; number instead of one per element. A sum is a weak check on its own -
; any bug that permutes elements passes - so tests pair it with indexed
; reads at the boundaries. `vecHash` is the position-sensitive companion
; for when order matters.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecSum (-> (Vec Int) Int))
(pub fn (vecSum v)
  (vecSumFrom v 0 0))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecSumFrom (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int))
(fn (vecSumFrom v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    (vecSumFrom v (+ i 1) (+ acc (vecGet v i)))))

; A position-sensitive digest of the whole vector.
;
; `h * 31 + x` per element, reduced mod a prime small enough that
; `h * 31` cannot overflow `Int` (10^9 * 31 < 2^63). Unlike `vecSum` this
; changes when elements are reordered, which is what makes it usable as
; the single assertion in a test that cares about order.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecHash (-> (Vec Int) Int))
(pub fn (vecHash v)
  (vecHashFrom v 0 1))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecHashFrom (-> (Vec Int) Int Int Int))
(fn (vecHashFrom v i h)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    h
    (vecHashFrom v (+ i 1) (% (+ (* h 31) (vecGet v i)) 1000000007))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Ordering
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A sort is a PERMUTATION, and that is why these move words through
; `memGetWord`/`memSetWord` on the data buffer rather than through
; `vecSet`.
;
; `vecSet` releases the element it displaces on a vector that owns its elements,
; which is right for a write and wrong for a swap: the two halves of a
; swap displace each other, so the pair would release both shares and
; retain them back, and a count that touches zero between the two is a
; block handed to the next same-size allocation while this vector still
; addresses it. Moving the raw words takes no share and gives none back
; - a permutation changes no element's count, which is the whole reason
; it is safe to bypass the accessor here.
;
; HEAPSORT, not quicksort: it is in place, it has no recursion to grow
; a stack with, and its worst case is its average case. A bump allocator
; with no `free` makes an out-of-place merge sort expensive in exactly
; the way this container exists to avoid, and quicksort's worst case is
; a sorted input, which is the input a caller most often has.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecSwapWords (-> Int Int Int Int))
(fn (vecSwapWords d i j)
  (let ((t (memGetWord d i)))
    {
      (memSetWord d i (memGetWord d j))
      (memSetWord d j t)
      0
    }))

; Restore the heap property at `root`, over the first `n` words.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(:: vecSiftDown (-> Int Int Int Int))
(fn (vecSiftDown d root n)
  (let (
    (mut r root)
    (mut go 1)
  )
    {
      (while (== go 1)
        (let ((c (+ (* 2 r) 1)))
          (if (>= c n)
            (set go 0)
            (let ((mut k c))
              {
                (if (< (+ c 1) n)
                  (if (> (memGetWord d (+ c 1)) (memGetWord d c))
                    (set k (+ c 1))
                    0)
                  0)
                (if (>= (memGetWord d r) (memGetWord d k))
                  (set go 0)
                  {
                    (vecSwapWords d r k)
                    (set r k)
                  })
              }))))
      0
    }))

; Sort a vector of `Int` ascending, in place. Answers the vector.
;
; The body orders the words the vector holds, which is numeric order
; only for an `Int`: the word of a `String` is a handle, and its order
; is an allocation order that means nothing. So the signature names
; `Int`, and any other element type sorts with `vecSortBy` and a
; comparison, such as `strCmp` for strings.
;@axiom:restrict(no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign)
(pub :: vecSort (-> (Vec Int) (Vec Int)))
(pub fn (vecSort v)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen v))
    (d (vecData v))
  )
    {
      (let ((mut i (- (/ n 2) 1)))
        (while (>= i 0)
          {
            (vecSiftDown d i n)
            (set i (- i 1))
          }))
      (let ((mut e (- n 1)))
        (while (> e 0)
          {
            (vecSwapWords d 0 e)
            (vecSiftDown d 0 e)
            (set e (- e 1))
          }))
      v
    }))

(:: vecSiftDownBy (-> Int Int Int (-> Int Int Int) Int))
(fn (vecSiftDownBy d root n cmp)
  (let (
    (mut r root)
    (mut go 1)
  )
    {
      (while (== go 1)
        (let ((c (+ (* 2 r) 1)))
          (if (>= c n)
            (set go 0)
            (let ((mut k c))
              {
                (if (< (+ c 1) n)
                  (if (> (cmp (memGetWord d (+ c 1)) (memGetWord d c)) 0)
                    (set k (+ c 1))
                    0)
                  0)
                (if (>= (cmp (memGetWord d r) (memGetWord d k)) 0)
                  (set go 0)
                  {
                    (vecSwapWords d r k)
                    (set r k)
                  })
              }))))
      0
    }))

; The same, ordered by a caller's comparison rather than by the word.
;
; `cmp` answers negative, zero or positive the way `strCmp` does, and
; it takes the WORDS the vector holds, because a `Vec` carries no
; element type. Ordering a vector of `String` by its bytes - which is
; what `vecSort` cannot do, since the word it holds for a `String` is
; an address - is therefore:
;
;   (vecSortBy v (lambda (a b) (strCmp (cast String a) (cast String b))))
;
; and not `(vecSortBy v strCmp)`: a top-level function is not a value
; in this language, because a partial application has no closure record
; to hold the arguments it was not given (`AX3013`). The `cast` is the
; caller saying what the word is, which is the same claim `vecGetStr`
; makes at the same boundary.
;
; SPELLED OUT RATHER THAN SHARED WITH `vecSort`. The two differ in one
; expression each, and folding them together means either `vecSort`
; pays an indirect call per comparison - about 60,000 of them for 5,000
; elements - or `vecSortBy` grows a branch per comparison to ask which
; it is. The duplication is thirty lines that say the same thing twice
; and never diverge in behaviour, because the fixture below sorts one
; input both ways and requires the same answer.
(pub :: vecSortBy (-> (Vec a) (-> Int Int Int) (Vec a)))
(pub fn (vecSortBy v cmp)
  (let (
    (n (vecLen v))
    (d (vecData v))
  )
    {
      (let ((mut i (- (/ n 2) 1)))
        (while (>= i 0)
          {
            (vecSiftDownBy d i n cmp)
            (set i (- i 1))
          }))
      (let ((mut e (- n 1)))
        (while (> e 0)
          {
            (vecSwapWords d 0 e)
            (vecSiftDownBy d 0 e cmp)
            (set e (- e 1))
          }))
      v
    }))
